    "result_text": "The full suite is green. \n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country national-number length validation for E.164 telephone numbers in the owner-creation/update pipeline.\n\n**Change** \u2014 `E164Telephone.java` (`src/main/java/.../rest/function/owner/E164Telephone.java`):\n- Added a `NATIONAL_LENGTHS` map (`\"61\"` \u2192 9, `\"1\"` \u2192 10) capturing the required national-number length per country code.\n- Added a private `hasValidNationalLength(digits)` helper that identifies the country code as the longest known prefix of the E.164 body and checks that the remaining national digits match the required length. Country codes with no known rule fall back to the general 8\u201315-digit bound (unchanged).\n- `format()` now returns `null` when the national length is wrong for a known country code, so it flows through the existing `NormalizeOwnerTelephone` \u2192 `InvalidTelephoneException` \u2192 `InvalidTelephoneExceptionHandler` path, which already responds **400** with `{\"errors\":[\"telephone\"]}`.\n\n**Why this placement:** `E164Telephone` already owns knowledge of E.164 structure (country code + national digits), so the length rule belongs there as a small, focused helper rather than a new class or a bolt-on in the pipeline step. No new exception, handler, or wiring was needed \u2014 I reused the existing 400 rejection path.\n\nBoth the new acceptance checkpoint (`errorRejectsWrongNationalLength`: `+61 123` \u2192 400) and the full suite pass. The `+61`-assumed happy path (`uniqueTelephone()` \u2192 `04XXXXXXXX` \u2192 9 national digits) and existing `+1` NANP seed data (10 national digits) both continue to satisfy the rule.",
